Helps that my main occupation is printing propaganda, which entails
fairly lengthy periods of waiting between gremlin attacks (our press is
possessed by evil demons, I'm sure.  We've even had to call in an
excorcist a few times to get rid of them.  Luckily they haven't plagued
us much of late).  Plus 2 hours on BART each day commuting.

This, plus the fact that I starve for reading material, which I devour
and digest fairly quickly and completely (although I vary my courses.
The guy at KFC where I get chicken noticed that I usually carry in a
different book each time.  The reason is that when it comes to tough
stuff, like Menger's "Investigations into the Methods of the Social
Sciences," I read a little bit at a time, then read a little bit of
something else, often interspersing fiction or history as light reading
- although I read Paul Johnson's "Birth of the Modern" as light reading!)
makes for my having read a wide range of things, although it also makes
me something of a jack of all intellectual trades and master of none.
The closest I've come to mastering any intellectual trade is philosophy,
which I know more about than most people, but less about than most
who have a degree in the subject, except for some specialized areas.

I seem to have above-usual recall, too, but I'm afraid chapter and
verse on the postal monopoly laws is a bit above me.  It's really not
complex enough to study in much depth.  I can flesh out Spooner's
challenge to the USPS, the American Letter Mail company a bit more:

Spooner was very entrepreneurial, and he formed this company in Boston
to compete in the letter delivery market with the USPS.  This was
recently after Jackson's refusal to renew the charter of the Second
Bank of the United States, so there was recent precedent for the defeat
of Federal agencies.  Spooner's case went all the way to the Supreme
Court, where he represented himself in his own inimitable style, and
he lost.  The USPS put him out of business, incorporating some of his
improvements, but still delivered worse service at a higher price.

Interestingly enough, Spooner's case against the USPS didn't rest
entirely on what would be called "economic" or "efficiency" grounds.  He
also pointed out that the USPS monopoly meant that agents of the Federal
State were placed in every State, county, and most other municipalities
of the country (at that time no one called it "the Nation" or "the Union."
"The Union" didn't come into currency until after the Civil War, and
"the Nation" didn't come into currency until the late 19th-century,
what Strauss and Howe call the "Missionary" Generation), with the
usual implications for civil liberties that implied.  Spooner was soon
proven right that the USPS presented a clear and present danger to
civil liberties when the first Federal censorship came about during the
Civil War - not against matters military, but pornographic!  Apparently,
there was much worry about what grunts and their sweethearts back home
were corresponding about :-).  I don't know if this was by act of
Congress or merely by executive order, but it preceded the Comstock
Laws, which are usually credited with being the first Federal censorship
legislation in the USA.  Enacted in the 1870s, as I recall, and protested
by civil disobedience by many of the leading libertarians of the time,
including Moses Harman and Ezra Heywood, if I recall what Wendy McElroy
wrote in her introduction to "Freedom, Feminism, and the State" aright.

Sorry, no chapter and verse, but that's about all I could come up with.

