Subject: Meaninglessness

Dave Burns:

>> "'existence exists' is meaningless [because non-operational]"
>> is a statement of how to test for meaningful content. [. . .]
> 
> What operational test would lend meaning to the above paragraph,
> the post it came from, or any of these claims?

I'm not sure if it requires an operational definition, since it is not
a description but a prescription.  It's concepts that require
operational definitions.

> 'Operationalization has been a useful heuristic in physics' 

Not all my examples were from physics, for instance the 'free-will'
versus 'will' example or the PS for an AI/CS example.  The operational
approach is useful in all categories of knowledge.  Maths statements or
propositions, for instance, are accepted by applying previously accepted
transformative rules/propositions to show that what is unclearly
true/false/undecidable is clearly true/false/undecidable.

> is not the same as 'that which is non-operational is meaningless.'

That is not quite what I said.  'A is A' is non-operational but
meaningful, although tautologous.  My point was that 'existence exists'
is not intended as a tautology (or perhaps it is?  Let me know and we
can stop talking:-), and so we must search for an operational
definition.

> In effect, my correspondent claims that most of the conversation on
> this list is some meaningless ritualized interaction

No, I would say:
 "most of the so-called _philosophical_ conversation on this list is
  some meaningless ritualized interaction"

>, and that if I claim to actually understand what someone writes,
> I'm lying or deluding myself - unless what they've said can be tested
> operationally.

I've not accused anyone of lying.  But other than that, yes, if someone
introduces a philosophical concept which is not capable of an
operational definition then they've added nothing, except perhaps a
tautology - which, by definition, is nothing new.  Anyone who thinks
they have is deluded.

> What is the operational definition of meaning?
>
> If I examine a sample of physics students performing identical
> experiments, what measurement would show which of them are using 
> the proper definition of meaning and which use a tainted version?

You have a strange notion of meaning.  Obviously no external, one-way
observation of "students performing identical experiments", can tell you
how they internalise concepts, since, by your own definition, they
appear/perform identically.  To find out how they understand what
something means I would _ask_ them, individually, to describe their
methodologies to me.

Dave enunciates my position as:

> "All statements are true, false, or meaningless. All true and false
> statements can be tested operationally. All statements that cannot be
> tested operationally are meaningless."

I would rather say:

     "All concepts are meaningless unless they can be defined
     operationally."

> For the above set of propositions to achieve truth by it's own
> criteria, it must be operationally testable. Is it true? What test
> reveals the truth?

The test is for you to define a concept _without_ defining it
operationally.  Have a go.  I don't believe it can be done.

>> What's good enough for Newton and Einstein is good enough for me
>> (there we are, a blatant, unabashed appeal to authority!). 
> 
> Me too. But neither of these authorities agrees with this notion of
> meaning.

On contrary, both were ardent operationalists at the height of their
creative periods.  Newton's "I frame no hypotheses" was a public retreat
from fruitless metaphysical speculation about gravitational action at
a distance.  Einstein was influenced by Mach when he devised special
relativity, although he later softened somewhat (correctly, IMO).

> An appeal to authority would have to go to Ernst Mach or the
> logical positivists.

I do not appeal to them, not being as extreme as Mach or the logical
positivists, who reject all concepts not directly tied to observation. 
I have no problems with concepts that are only indirectly tied to
observation.  What I have problems with are concepts that can't be
related to anything operational and, consequently, slide about like
loose cannons in our minds.

> Neither Newton nor Einstein shared this horror of metaphysics.

My horror is not of metaphysics but of fuzzy, woolly metaphysics.

> Dave Burns 

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

PS Here's an example from the list about 18 months ago, where I found
the requirement for operationally defined concepts very useful.  We were
collectively grappling with the idea of split-brain experiments,
copying, uploading etc and identity.  The problem was if some of our
copies were going to be tortured and some rewarded, how should we,
before copying, view this?  Specifically could we assign a probability
to waking up after the copying and being tortured, if we knew that a
certain proportion of the copies were to be so ill-treated?  At first
glance it seems straightforward.  Your chance of being tortured is the
number of copies tortured / total number of copies (incl. original). 
I won't recap the whole thread, but this notion broke down under
scrutiny and is self-contradictory.  I only solved the problem to my own
satisfaction when I realised that this notion of probability was not
operationally meaningful.  Not only was it unmeasurable to an external
observer, but the subject(s) couldn't measure it either.  Before the
copying it had no meaning to the single subject and afterwards each copy
is indifferent to the experiences of the other copies.  So I was forced
to discard this intuitive notion of probability applied to the stream
of consciousness.

A lot of the puzzles about identity are amenable to this sort of
approach.

