Date: Tue, 21 Sep 93 09:02:00 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: SPACE: DC-X Flies

Re: long term potential of electromagnetic launch

Consider an electromagnetic catapult (of which maglev is a special
case) with an ability to launch 100 kg payloads at intervals of 1
minute.  At 9 km/s launch velocity you should have enough left after
atmospheric drag losses to be in orbit (about 7.9 km/s).  Allowing
for a couple of inches of ablative loss on the leading edge of the
projectile, and the coils on what you are throwing, I shall assume
you have a 125 kg projectile.  You need 5.06 gigaJoules of kinetic
energy in the projectile at launch (1/2mv^2).  Electromagnetic
launchers are estimated to cost on the order of $10/kilojoule total
to construct, which gives a $50 million projected cost for the 
launcher.  Firing the launcher every minute you get 500,000 shots
a year.  Assuming that depreciation and maintenance of the launcher
is 20% of the capital cost per year, you spend $20 per shot
on this, or $0.20 per kg payload.  With 85% efficiency, you need to
input 6 gigajoules from the power line, or 1667 kWh.  At $0.08/kWh,
electric cost is $133.33 per shot.  If we assume the projectiles
are built at the cost/kg of automobiles (the production rates are
similar), we spend $10/kg on the projectile, or $250 per shot.

Thus, the total launch cost is $403.33 per shot, or $4.03 per kg,
about 1000 times less than we spend now.  I should point out that
we are a long way from demonstrating an electromagnetic catapult
that throws large projectiles at 9 km/s, much less reliably for
years at a time, but this gives you an idea of where the limits
of the technology lie.

Comparison with rockets:

In theory, a conventional two stage reuseable rocket should be able
to fly many times between major maintenance, just like airplanes
do.  If produced in similar quantities, rockets should cost about
the same as aircraft to produce per kg.  A 747 costs $900 per kg to
buy.  If you have 10 kg of rocket per kg payload delivered, then
you have $9000 of rocket hardware/kg payload.  Assuming that you
spend 20% on depreciation and maintenance/year, and get 100 flights
per year from a vehicle, you expend $18/kg launched.  Propellants
add another $20/kg to this, for a total launch cost of around $40/kg.

