From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: Realism & Idealism: Bishop Berkeley 

starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu says:
> Berkeley's a good example of rationalism based upon an unsound premise.
> He took "esse is percipi," or "to be is to be perceived" to be axiomatic,
> made it his foundational premise, and reasoned validly from there to
> false conclusions.  Problem is, "essi is percipi" doesn't pass the test of
> axioms: its denial doesn't entail its presupposition.

Tim is apparently not reading my posts any more, but for those of you
who know logic, you should know that an axiom can not be tested, and
its denial does not entail its presupposition. Take, for example, the
parallel lines postulate. I suspect non-euclidian geometry isn't much
loved by the objectivists.

Axioms are arbitrary. The best one can say is that a set of axioms are
inconsistant -- you can't even demonstrate that they are consistant!
(See Goedel for that one.) You can't know what is and isn't "really"
an axiom. You basically have to test the system generated by the
axioms you picked against the real world and see what works. For
example, over short distances, non-euclidean geometries don't seem to
give the right answers, so our ancestors who needed the math to do
surveying picked euclidean axioms. That doesn't mean, however, that
they were necessarily perfectly right -- there is some evidence that
over long distances space is curved. 

This is a typical example, by the way, of where the "Objectivist
disease" shows up -- I suspect that objectivists would have too much
attachment to their axioms to understand that the system they had
devised was merely a model, and might not be correct if some of their
assumptions (short distances, speed much lower than light, etc) no
longer held.

